red together  at the center over here we can see how  this expression profiling  this this is a hierarchical clustering  unsupervised but you can see the ccs  case and the fusion positive tumors with  vcor  all clustered together neatly so that's  a remarkable finding  now we know about five or six years down  the line  that many of these tumors that we call  non-deficient  sarcomas and other tumors can have  both types of alterations defining this  tumor so this table over here at the  bottom shows  on the first column you have the  different tumor types  the second column shows the incidence of  b  core fusion in these tumors and then the  third column shows the incidence of  itds in these tumors and what we found  was that  somewhat interchangeably most of these  tumors can have either one  and so this is borne out by the genetic  and by the expression studies that i've  shown you so far  so changing gears over here for the last  few minutes  i'm going to focus on again an  unexpected finding but in this case  not of any particular mutation or any  particular structural variation  but in broadly of the mutation rate or  mutation load in a tumor  so we refer to this as hypermutation  this is one patient from our archive who  we published about a couple of years  back  this patient presented to our service  with um what is typically referred to as  a multiple  cafe all this pots and calculate spots  are nothing but  a portion of skin discoloration you can  see in in figure a  and figure b in panel a and panel b  you can see multiple areas of the back  of the head as well as the  the back of the patient showing dark  discolored  patches these are uh often referred to  as caffeiolic spots  and and in a patient with so many  caffeine spots  uh a typical clinical impression is of  potentially  of a neurofibromatosis or neurofibroma  but because of the presence of multiple  of these and the patient presented with  the large tumor which is seen in c and d  in the brain o